WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.260
A mountain warms by 0.3 °C each decade.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.640
How far uphill must the beetles' range shift, to stay as cool as before?

3
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:18.633
Picture the slope as stacked bands of temperature, warm at the bottom and cool at

4
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:23.893
the top. The gold line is an isotherm, a line of equal temperature.

5
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:27.663
This beetle lives only where it is cool enough.

6
00:00:28.943 --> 00:00:32.563
So the gold line marks the lower edge of its range, the warm edge.

7
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.387
Now warm the whole mountain.

8
00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:40.307
Every band slides upward, and the gold line climbs with them.

9
00:00:41.447 --> 00:00:43.127
The beetle's climate is on the move.

10
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:45.870
How fast does the line climb?

11
00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.050
That depends on how quickly air cools with height, a slope's lapse rate.

12
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.510
Here it cools 0.6 °C every 100 metres.

13
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.000
Divide the warming rate by the lapse rate.

14
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.920
The result is the climate velocity, the speed of the isotherm.

15
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.040
On this slope, it is fifty metres uphill each decade.

16
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.910
Now let the population move, as beetles settle higher and low ones die out.

17
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.970
The green band climbs, but slower than the gold line.

18
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:22.133
The red bracket between band and line is the gap.

19
00:01:23.273 --> 00:01:25.453
Each decade the line pulls further ahead.

20
00:01:26.593 --> 00:01:31.533
If the beetles started in step, the gap is the difference in speeds, times the time.

21
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:35.247
So moving is not the same as keeping up.

22
00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:39.587
A range keeps up only by moving as fast as its isotherm.

23
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.810
Now set a flat plain beside it.

24
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.930
There, temperature changes toward the pole, and far more slowly with distance.

25
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:54.223
So on the flat, how far toward the pole gives the same cooling as one metre uphill?

26
00:01:55.623 --> 00:01:58.363
A metre, a hundred metres, or about a kilometre?

27
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:08.307
In one European example, about a kilometre and a half.

28
00:02:09.547 --> 00:02:12.107
A short climb buys the cooling of a long journey.

29
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:15.107
The exchange rate varies by place.

30
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.520
So raw distances cannot say which shift kept up.

31
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.820
Each shift is judged against its own isotherm, on its own slope or plain.

32
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.770
A 2011 synthesis pooled many studies of land species.

33
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:36.310
The median shifts were about 17 kilometres poleward and 11 metres uphill each

34
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.310
decade.

35
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:40.213
But the medians hide wide variation.

36
00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:45.533
Some species ran ahead of their isotherm, and many lagged behind it.

37
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:52.797
One last warning. For a forest beetle, clearing the lower slope can lift the range's

38
00:02:52.797 --> 00:02:55.197
bottom edge, whatever the temperature does.

39
00:02:56.457 --> 00:02:58.337
A shift shows change, not its cause.

40
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:03.037
A range keeps up only by moving as fast as its isotherm.
